    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Mold spores range from about 1 to 30 microns, which is exactly the size a true HEPA filter catches most reliably. So almost any well-built HEPA purifier sized for your room will reduce airborne spores. What separates the good choices is airflow for the space, a real carbon bed for odor, honest filter costs, and the absence of ozone-producing gimmicks.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Air Purifiers for Mold, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Each pick uses a true HEPA or HEPA-grade filter, and the rankings draw on published CADR figures, AHAM Verified and CARB listings, filter replacement costs, and patterns in owner feedback. No hands-on lab testing is claimed. Where the maker quotes room coverage, treat it as a rough guide and size up for mold work.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-can-do" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What an Air Purifier Can and Cannot Do About Mold
          </h2>

          <p>
            An air purifier is a fan pushing room air through filters. If those filters are true HEPA, they capture at least 99.97 percent of particles at 0.3 microns, the size HEPA filters find hardest to catch, and they are even more effective on larger particles. Mold spores are typically 1 to 30 microns, so a sealed HEPA purifier removes most of the spores that pass through it. That lowers what you breathe and can noticeably reduce allergy symptoms.
          </p>

          <p>
            What it cannot do is reach mold where it lives. Colonies grow on damp drywall, behind baseboards, on the underside of subfloors, and in carpet pads. A purifier only cleans air that flows into it, and a growing colony keeps releasing new spores. Running a purifier without fixing the damp is like running a mop under a dripping pipe. It helps, but it never finishes.
          </p>

          <p>
            That makes the purifier the third step, not the first. Step one is moisture control, usually with a dehumidifier from our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best basement dehumidifiers</a> guide. Step two is cleaning or removing visible growth with a product chosen from our <a href="/best-mold-remover/">mold remover roundup</a>. Step three is a purifier that keeps airborne spores down while the space dries and during the cleanup itself, when disturbed mold sheds spores in large numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            A purifier also helps with the smell, but only if it has a meaningful amount of activated carbon. The musty odor is made of microbial volatile organic compounds, gases that a particle filter cannot catch. A thin carbon sheet handles light odor; a deep carbon bed, measured in pounds rather than ounces, handles persistent smell.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Fix the damp first, then filter the air</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Mold cannot grow without moisture. Keep relative humidity below about 50 percent and the colony stops spreading. The purifier then catches what is left in the air instead of fighting a source that keeps producing spores.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Specs That Actually Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean air delivery rate, or CADR, is the number to start with. It measures how many cubic feet of filtered air a purifier delivers per minute, tested by AHAM for smoke, dust, and pollen. For mold, the dust and pollen figures are the most relevant because spores sit in that size range. A unit marked AHAM Verified has had its CADR checked by an independent lab, which makes comparisons between brands more trustworthy.
          </p>

          <p>
            True HEPA matters more than marketing variants. Labels like HEPA-type, HEPA-like, or 99 percent HEPA are not the same standard. Look for language stating 99.97 percent capture at 0.3 microns, or an equivalent medical-grade rating. A good filter in a leaky housing still lets air bypass the media, so sealed designs with a gasket around the filter perform closer to their rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carbon weight tells you how much odor a unit can handle. Most compact purifiers carry a few ounces of carbon in a thin sheet, which saturates fast in a musty room. Units built for odor and chemicals carry pounds. If the manufacturer does not list carbon weight, assume it is light.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
