              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Finally, check CARB certification. California limits ozone output from indoor air cleaners, and any unit sold there must meet that limit. Certification does not guarantee zero ozone, but it rules out the worst offenders.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing a Purifier for a Mold Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            AHAM's rule of thumb pairs a purifier's smoke CADR with a room of about 1.5 times that figure in square feet, which works out to roughly five air changes per hour in a room with eight-foot ceilings. For allergy and mold work, aim for at least that and preferably more. In plain numbers, a 300 square foot bedroom wants a CADR of 200 or more, and a 600 square foot basement den wants 400 or more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manufacturer coverage claims are often based on a single air change per hour, which is fine for general freshness but too low for spores. If a box says it covers 1,500 square feet, check the fine print, then divide by three to four to get a realistic mold-control coverage. This is the most common reason people buy a purifier, run it for a month, and decide purifiers do not work.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Room size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Minimum CADR</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fits</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Up to 200 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Winix 5500-2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">200-400 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 260</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Winix 5500-2, Levoit Core 600S</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">400-650 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Levoit Core 600S, Blueair 211+, Coway Airmega 400</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">650-1,000 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">600 or two units</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Coway Airmega 400, or two mid-size units</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Odor-first rooms</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Any of the above plus heavy carbon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Austin Air HealthMate Plus, IQAir HealthPro Plus</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two mid-size units often beat one large unit in a chopped-up basement, because each one cleans the air in its own zone. A single large purifier in a corner of an L-shaped space leaves the far end with little circulation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ceiling height matters too. A basement with seven-foot ceilings has less air than a room with nine-foot ceilings, so a given CADR goes further. A great room with vaulted ceilings needs more than the floor area alone suggests.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="avoid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Ozone, UV, and Ionizers: What to Skip
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ozone generators are sold as mold killers, and that claim does not hold up at safe concentrations. The EPA states that ozone at levels that do not harm people has little effect on mold and other contaminants, and that ozone itself irritates the lungs and can worsen asthma. Ozone shock treatments of empty buildings exist in professional practice, but they are not a household appliance feature. Do not run an ozone generator in an occupied home.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ionizers and electrostatic features charge particles so they cling to surfaces or collector plates. Some produce small amounts of ozone as a by-product. Many reputable purifiers include one with an off switch and CARB certification, which is a reasonable compromise. If anyone in the house has asthma, leave it off; the HEPA filter does the work either way.
          </p>

          <p>
            UV-C lamps inside purifiers draw a lot of marketing. In principle UV-C damages microorganisms, but killing mold spores takes a substantial dose, and air flows past a small internal lamp in a fraction of a second. Spores caught on a HEPA filter are already trapped and do not grow in a dry filter. A UV lamp is not harmful when sealed inside a certified unit, but it is not a reason to pay more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Photocatalytic oxidation and similar technologies can generate formaldehyde and other by-products under some conditions. For a mold problem, they add cost and uncertainty without solving the core issue. A sealed true HEPA filter, a meaningful carbon bed, and enough airflow for the room is the formula that works.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Placement and How Long to Run It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Put the purifier in the room where people spend the most time, not necessarily where the mold was. If the family room is in the basement, put it there. If the basement is storage and the problem is spores drifting upstairs, a unit in the main living area or bedroom often does more for your lungs. Keep it at least a foot from walls and furniture so it can pull air freely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run it continuously. Spore levels rebound within an hour or two of switching a purifier off, especially in a basement where something is still releasing them. Auto mode is fine as long as the sensor sees particles; many owners run the fan on low or medium overnight for quiet and let auto take over during the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            During cleanup, run a purifier in the work area or just outside it, with the door closed, and keep it running for a day or two after you finish. Scrubbing, sanding, or removing drywall releases a burst of spores, and a purifier capturing them limits how many settle elsewhere in the house. Do not let it replace a proper respirator, which you should wear during any mold cleanup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Close windows while it runs in humid weather. Outdoor air brings outdoor spores, and in summer it also brings moisture that undoes the dehumidifier. In dry, cool weather, airing a basement out briefly can help, but the purifier is most effective in a closed room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="purifier-vs-dehumidifier" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Purifier or Dehumidifier: Which Comes First
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you can only afford one machine for a damp, musty basement, buy the dehumidifier. It removes the moisture that lets mold grow, and over a few weeks spore production drops along with humidity. A purifier without humidity control is permanently playing catch-up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy the purifier first only when the moisture is already under control and symptoms persist, or when the concern is a specific room, such as a bedroom with a history of mold, where you want cleaner air immediately while the larger fix is underway. People with mold allergies often benefit from both, run together, for the first month after a cleanup.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two machines also work differently in winter. Basements are usually drier in cold weather, so the dehumidifier may barely run. The purifier keeps catching dust and any residual spores year-round, which makes it the machine that stays on longer over the course of a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            A good pairing for a typical 1,000 square foot basement is a 50-pint Energy Star dehumidifier and a mid-size HEPA purifier with a CADR of around 250 to 400. Total cost runs roughly $450 to $750 at the time of writing, which is less than many single remediation visits and keeps working long after.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="filter-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Filter Costs and How Often to Change Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            The purchase price is only part of the cost. Replacement filters for the picks here range from roughly $40 a year for budget models to well over $150 a year for large-room and medical-grade units, depending on how hard they run. Check filter price and availability before buying; a purifier whose filter goes unreplaced eventually just circulates air.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Annual Filter Cost</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$40-$70</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Winix 5500-2</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$60-$100</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Levoit Core 600S</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$100-$150</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Coway Airmega 400</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$80-$150</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Austin or IQAir, averaged</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Change HEPA filters on the maker's schedule or sooner during a mold cleanup. The filter holds captured spores, and while they generally do not grow on a dry filter, a filter that has been in a damp basement for a long time can develop a smell. Bag the old filter outdoors and dispose of it with household trash.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pre-filters are worth cleaning every two to four weeks in a basement. They catch lint, pet hair, and dust before it clogs the more expensive HEPA layer, which extends its life. Vacuum or rinse them according to the manual, and let washable ones dry fully before you put them back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carbon is the stage that wears out fastest in a musty room. Once it saturates, it stops absorbing odor and the smell comes back. If the musty smell returns while the purifier is still running, the carbon is likely spent, even if the HEPA stage has life left.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="room-setups" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Practical Setups for Common Rooms
          </h2>

          <p>
            Finished basement family room: pair a 50-pint dehumidifier with a large-room purifier such as the Coway Airmega 400 or Levoit Core 600S. Put the purifier near the seating area and the dehumidifier near the dampest wall or the sump pit. Keep the stair door closed during humid months so upstairs air does not pull basement spores up the stairwell, and so the dehumidifier is not trying to dry the whole house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bedroom above a damp crawlspace or basement: a mid-size true HEPA unit like the Winix 5500-2 running on low through the night is usually enough. Seal gaps around plumbing penetrations in the floor with foam or caulk, because those openings are a direct path for crawlspace air. If you wake up congested and feel better away from home, look at the crawlspace before buying a second purifier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bathroom with recurring mildew: a purifier is the wrong tool here. Bathroom mold is a ventilation problem, and the fix is an exhaust fan that vents outdoors and runs for 20 to 30 minutes after every shower, plus regular cleaning of grout and caulk. Save the purifier budget for a room where people spend hours at a time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storage basement nobody uses: if the space is unfinished and you rarely go down there, a dehumidifier alone is usually the better investment. Spores that stay in the basement do little harm to people upstairs, especially with the stair door closed. If basement air does reach the living space through return ducts in the basement, change the furnace filter to a MERV 11 to 13 rated filter, which many residential systems can accept without restricting airflow too much; check with your HVAC installer if unsure, and check that return ducts are sealed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="is-it-working" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Tell Whether the Purifier Is Helping
          </h2>

          <p>
            The honest answer is that you cannot see spores, so judge results by what you can observe. Within a week or two of running a correctly sized unit continuously, most people with mold allergies notice fewer morning symptoms, less congestion after time in the room, and less dust settling on flat surfaces. If none of that changes after a month, the purifier is too small, the source is still active, or the symptoms have a different cause.
          </p>

          <p>
            An inexpensive particle monitor helps. Consumer PM2.5 sensors do not count mold spores specifically, but they show whether the purifier is pulling particle levels down and how fast they rebound when it is off. Many smart purifiers, including the Levoit and Coway picks here, display this data in their apps. A room that drops quickly and stays low is getting enough air changes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the smell over several weeks. If the musty odor fades while the dehumidifier holds humidity below 50 percent, the combination is working. If the smell lingers even with dry air and a fresh carbon filter, something is still growing, often behind drywall, under flooring, or inside a wall cavity next to a plumbing leak. A moisture meter on the suspect wall is the next step.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the filter at the first change. A HEPA filter that comes out gray after a few months in a basement is doing its job. A filter that looks barely used after the same period suggests the unit is running too low, is blocked by furniture, or is in a room where little air moves. Adjust placement and fan speed before deciding the purifier is not up to the task.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pro-help" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. When the Problem Is Bigger Than a Purifier
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mold covering more than about 10 square feet, roughly a three-by-three-foot patch, is the point at which the EPA suggests consulting professional guidance. Growth inside HVAC ducts, behind finished walls after a flood, or anywhere water entered from sewage should be handled by a remediation contractor regardless of size.
          </p>

          <p>
            Persistent health symptoms are another trigger. If someone in the house has worsening asthma, frequent respiratory infections, or allergic reactions that track with time spent at home, see a doctor and consider a professional moisture and mold assessment. A purifier helps, but finding the hidden source matters more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before calling anyone, check the obvious water sources yourself. Gutters, downspouts, grading, plumbing leaks, and foundation cracks cause most basement moisture, and our guide on <a href="/how-to-fix-a-damp-basement/">fixing a damp basement</a> walks through them in order. A contractor will look at the same things first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be wary of companies that sell air testing as the main service and then recommend expensive treatments based on spore counts alone. Indoor air always contains some spores. The useful findings are moisture sources and visible or hidden growth, which a good inspector identifies with a moisture meter, a flashlight, and sometimes a thermal camera.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the coverage on the box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coverage claims are often based on one air change per hour, which suits general freshness but not spore control. A unit sold for 1,000 square feet may really clean 300 square feet at the rate mold control needs. Size from CADR instead, aiming for about two-thirds of the room area or more.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an ozone generator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ozone is marketed as a mold killer, but at levels safe for people it does little to mold and it irritates the lungs. The EPA advises against using ozone generators in occupied spaces. Spend the money on a sealed true HEPA unit and a dehumidifier instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting HEPA to remove the smell</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The musty smell is a gas, and HEPA filters only catch particles. A purifier with a thin carbon sheet will barely touch a strong basement odor. Choose a unit with a substantial carbon bed, and fix the moisture so the smell stops being produced in the first place.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it only when it smells</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Spore counts climb back within hours after a purifier shuts off. Switching it on only when the room smells bad means it is always starting from behind. Run it continuously on low or auto, and turn it up during cleanup or when the basement is in heavy use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping filter changes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A clogged or saturated filter moves less air, and a spent carbon stage lets the odor return. Owners often blame the purifier when the real issue is a filter overdue by months. Note the change date on the filter and budget the replacement cost when you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a HEPA purifier capture mold spores?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. True HEPA filters capture at least 99.97 percent of particles at 0.3 microns and an even higher share of larger particles, and mold spores are roughly 1 to 30 microns. A sealed HEPA purifier sized for the room will remove most airborne spores that pass through it. It will not remove mold growing on surfaces, so the source still has to be cleaned.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What CADR do I need for a musty room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aim for a dust or pollen CADR of at least two-thirds of the room's floor area in square feet, which gives several air changes per hour. A 300 square foot bedroom needs about 200, and a 600 square foot basement den needs about 400. For high ceilings or open floor plans, go higher or use two units.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does an air purifier get rid of mildew odor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it has enough activated carbon. The smell is made of gases released by mold, and HEPA filters do not catch gases. Units with a heavy carbon bed, like the Austin Air HealthMate Plus, handle persistent odor far better than units with a thin carbon sheet. Removing the moisture and growth stops the smell at its source.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should an air purifier run all day for mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, run it continuously. Spore levels rebuild within a couple of hours after a purifier is switched off, especially in a basement with any ongoing growth. Use auto mode or a low setting overnight and higher speeds during the day or during cleanup. The electricity cost of most purifiers on low is small.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a dehumidifier or air purifier better for mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For preventing mold, a dehumidifier is more important because it removes the moisture mold needs to grow. A purifier then catches spores already in the air and helps with allergies. In a damp basement, buy the dehumidifier first. If symptoms persist after humidity stays below about 50 percent, add a purifier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do UV-C air purifiers kill mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A small UV-C lamp inside a purifier has limited effect on mold spores because air passes it too quickly for a lethal dose. Spores trapped on a HEPA filter are already removed from the air. UV-C is not harmful in a sealed, certified unit, but the HEPA filter does the meaningful work, so do not pay extra for the lamp.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a purifier replace mold remediation?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A purifier lowers airborne spores but does nothing to colonies on walls, wood, or carpet, which keep releasing spores. Clean small areas yourself with the right product and protection. For growth larger than about 10 square feet, contamination in ducts, or mold after sewage or flood water, hire a qualified remediation contractor.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

